00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:06.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  back now at 7 30 on cbs mornings and as  we prepare to mark the anniversary of  the january 6th assault on the u.s  capitol we are hearing for the first  time from a top intelligence official in  the capitol police three days before the  attack she wrote an intelligence  assessment sounding the alarm about  potential violence at the capitol  much of which actually came to pass so  why were her warnings not acted upon  chris van cleave is on capitol hill with  an interview you will only see on cbs  mornings and it's a good one chris good  morning to you  good morning julie farnam had been on  the job just 72 days when rioters  stormed the capitol on january 6th but  it was this report she gave to capitol  police leadership three days earlier on  january third that's become a glaring  example of intelligence slipping through  the cracks  do you feel that that you and your team  bear responsibility for what happened on  the 6th no i don't i think we provided  the information i think we did an  excellent job julie farnam is the acting  intelligence director for the capitol  police for weeks leading up to january 6  she says her team had been gathering  intelligence indicating violence was  likely we knew there were going to be  thousands of protesters and we knew  there were going to be extremists there  and i knew things were not going to be  good that day cbs news obtained her  final intelligence assessment dated  january 3rd bottom line it read  protesters plan to be armed then there's  this on page 13 her team's overall  analysis which raised concerns about  permits given to groups affiliated with  stop the steel known to attract white  supremacists and militia members who  actively promote violence it warned  trump supporters see january 6 as the  last opportunity to overturn the results  of the presidential election this sense  of desperation and disappointment may  lead to more of an incentive to become  violent and unlike previous protests  congress itself is the target on the  sixth there was no other agency in the  federal government except the capitol  police that wrote a comprehensive  assessment and report that outlined the  violence that was expected that day how  do you justify the performance to some  of the folks that look at the end result  on january 6th  which was an utter failure  i think in some regards on january 6 our  intelligence division was an island we  bear some responsibility for that so  that the warnings were there they just  didn't get where they needed to go at  the end of the day  that's my understanding once i sent it  up to my leadership i didn't have  control over where it went last february  congress asked yogananda pittman who  became acting chief after the  insurrection about the january third  assessment although we knew the  likelihood for violence by extremists no  credible threat indicated that tens of  thousands would attack the u.s capitol  pittman is now back in the job she held  on january 6 overseeing all intelligence  operations for the capitol police  including julie farnham's team i think  that there will be people that see this  interview and wonder  should anyone who is involved with  intelligence before january 6th still be  involved with intelligence after january  6. i can't speak to the personnel issues  should you still be running the team yes  i should be because i largely wrote that  assessment and i'm the one who has been  working tirelessly every single day  since i've come on board to right the  ship the department declined to make  pittman available for an interview  current chief tom manger took over the  force in july why wasn't that  intelligence acted on i can't answer  that question because i was not here on  january 3rd nor january 6th but i can  tell you this that the way intelligence  was shared the way it was disseminated  needed vast improvement and so today  that kind of intelligence would be acted  on we're in a posture now where we're  not taking any chances what  responsibility does the department bear  for what happened that day i think that  overall the department bears the  majority of the responsibility i mean  the organization let the men and women  of this police department down they had  the intelligence the intelligence was  right but the leadership i think failed  to rank and foul members and they failed  congress congressman tim ryan chairs the  committee overseeing the capitol police  do you feel like the department has made  the necessary changes to prevent a  repeat of that day i'm not concerned  that there's going to be another  insurrection at the capitol we've take  taken some positive steps in the right  direction but there's a hell of a long  way for us to go over the last year the  department has opened its first ever  field offices the intelligence unit has  doubled in size and improved the  training of its analysts in an effort to  ensure something like the sixth never  happens again  is it hard for an intel person who's  delivered a report  much of it came to fruition  to then see the result on january 6th of  course it is i mean that was a difficult  day to see what happened i was listening  to the police radio when that was  happening and to hear  the officer's screams  and everything that happened that day  it's very difficult  chief manger is set to testify for the  first time before congress he will tell  the senate rules committee that january  6 exposed key deficiencies in the  capitol police and operational planning  staffing equipment and of course  intelligence he will lay out the ongoing  efforts to fix those deficiencies nate  chris thank you what we do know is the  information was shared  it just wasn't spread properly and then  passed up to the i also know that none  of this  undermines the heroism of the capitol  police rank and file who were there  trying to defend the building well the  resources that they were provided  it's so frustrating to hear that they  had access to the information it wasn't  acted upon i can only imagine how  frustrated she is and i get it gets to  me too when you see that police officer  screaming the way he was screaming right  but you would think it's not possible  what happened at the capitol that it  happened i still when i look at the  video i still can't believe what i'm  watching a year later we are better than  this yes it's americans against  americans  yes it does like i said tony in hopes  that it doesn't happen again yeah that's  for sure

